course: dictionary
lesson: 14691
title: Prevailing: the most common
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Prevailing means most common or strongest at a certain time or place. You can often work it out from the words around it. «The prevailing wind here blows from the south-west.» Winds that blow again and again from one direction are the most common winds, so prevailing must mean most common.
explain: The word prevail is a verb, and it has forms you will see in reading: prevails, prevailed and prevailing. To prevail also means to win in the end. «In the end, kindness prevailed.» Kindness won out in the end, so prevailed means won.
explain: Test your guess by putting an easier word in place of the hard one. «The prevailing opinion in the class was that the trip should go ahead.» Could you say «the most common opinion»? Yes, it still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
fact: Prevailing means most common or strongest at a certain time or place.
fact: «The prevailing wind» is the wind that blows most often from one direction in a place.
fact: In the UK and Ireland, the prevailing wind is from the south-west.
fact: «The prevailing opinion» means what most people think.
fact: Prevail is a verb, and its forms are prevails, prevailed and prevailing. When someone prevails, they win in the end.
exercise: «The prevailing wind here blows from the south-west.» What does prevailing mean? | Quiet and still | Coming from the north | *Most common or strongest | Very cold and icy
exercise: «In the end, the good team prevailed and won the cup.» What does prevailed mean? | *Won in the end | Fell asleep on the bus | Gave up early | Got lost on the way
exercise: Which phrase means what most people think? | a quiet corner | a sudden storm | the broken window | *the prevailing opinion
test: What is a prevailing wind? | A wind that only blows at night | *A wind that blows most often from one direction in a place | A wind that blows during a storm only | A wind that never blows at all
test: Which word is a form of prevail? | prevent | pretend | *prevails | preview
exam: Ella checked the «prevailing conditions» before the hike. What does prevailing conditions mean? | A map of the hills | A long rope for climbing | *The weather and situation that are usual at that time | The shoes you wear on the hill
